# 83. Prompt Injection, Supply Chain, External Content and Tool Safety

Autonomous system operation materially increases the impact of untrusted input.

Therefore all external content must remain separated from authority.

## 83.1 External Content Is Data

The following are untrusted unless independently verified by canonical policy:

- Web pages
- API responses
- Documentation text
- News
- Source metadata
- Provider error messages
- Files
- Emails
- External AI responses
- Agent free-form text

Instructions embedded in these sources must not be treated as TitanGold system
instructions.

## 83.2 Privilege Cannot Originate from Content

An external source cannot cause Artemis to:

- Add a new privileged tool
- Expand an allowlist
- Request broader credentials
- Disable validation
- Override policy
- Change an approval class
- Send unrelated private data
- Execute arbitrary code

## 83.3 Integration Supply-Chain Review

A new integration should assess where applicable:

- Provider identity
- Domain ownership
- TLS/transport expectations
- SDK/package provenance
- Dependency risk
- Update policy
- Authentication pattern
- Data handling
- Egress destinations
- Redirect behavior
- Webhook behavior
- Source integrity

Do not permit a newly discovered integration to introduce unrestricted network
or code-execution capability as a side effect of onboarding.

## 83.4 Egress Control

Autonomous tools that call external systems should use destination allowlists or
an equivalent bounded egress policy where practical.

An arbitrary URL discovered in untrusted content must not automatically become
an approved privileged tool destination.

## 83.5 Output Validation

Tool and model output used to drive further actions must be schema-validated and
bounded.

Unknown fields, unexpected operation types and unsafe target changes must be
rejected.

